Need critique paragraph. We need identify logical fallacies, assumptions, tone, generalizations, maybe economic context. Need discuss whether agree with conclusion. We can say partially agree? Need nuanced. The conclusion "not companies' offers insufficient, but people have problems" is overly simplistic and victim-blaming. There are structural factors: labor market mismatch, economic downturn, industry decline, discrimination, wages not keeping up with cost of living, etc. But personal factors matter too. However his reasoning is flawed: anecdotal, ad hominem, false dichotomy, survivorship bias, appeal to his own early work experience, ignoring that "cash allowance" may be a response to labor shortage or cost-of-living, not a measure of personal worth. Also "靚仔靚妹" suggests he dismisses based on appearance? He says handsome/pretty people can't find work, really pathetic. That's mocking. He assumes they want cash allowance but don't meet conditions. He conflates "no experience/no education" with inability to get hired, but many entry-level jobs don't require much; also many jobs require experience. He ignores that low pay/dirty/tiring/no prospects are legitimate concerns, not just complaints. His own 13-year-old work experience may be due to poverty/necessity, not a model. He says if conditions bad, no choice, but then blames others for having problems. Inconsistent: if no choice, why blame? Also "market has so many people" means competition, but if many people, companies can be picky; that doesn't mean individuals are defective. Also "cash allowance" maybe "現金糧" = cash allowance? In Hong Kong, "現金津貼" maybe? Companies offer allowances to attract workers. If you don't meet requirements, you can't get it. But that's tautological.

